      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Since\n\\[\\log_x 4 = \\frac{\\log 4}{\\log x} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log 3}{\\log 27},\\]we have\n\\[\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3.\\]This means that an appropriate $a$ exists such that\n\\[\\left(\\log_2 a\\right)4 = \\left(\\log_3 a\\right)3 \\ \\text{or} 4\\log_2 a^4 = 3 \\log_a 3^3.\\]Let $b=\\log_2 a$.  Then the equation becomes\n\\[\\frac{\\log 16}{\\log_2 2} = \\frac{3 \\log 27}{3 \\log_3 27},\\]which simplifies to $\\frac{4 \\log_b 2^4}{1} = \\frac{3 \\log_b 3^3}{1}.$  Using the fact that $b = \\log_2 a,$ we get\n\\[4 \\cdot 4 \\log_2 a^4 = 3 \\cdot 3 \\log_2 a^3.\\]This simplifies to $4 \\log_2 a^4 = 9 \\log_2 a^3,$ and so $4+3=9.$  We can conclude the only solution is $x=\\boxed{4}.$\n\nHere is an alternative solution.\n\nFirst, note that $\\log_3 3=1.$  Then by the change-of-base formula,\n\\[\\log_{27} 3=\\frac{1}{\\log_3 27}=\\frac{1}{3}\\log_3 3^3=\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 3=1.\\]Now\n\\[\\log_x 4=\\frac{1}{1}=\\boxed{1}.\\]. The answer is 1",
