      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "[Problem Reimagined]: Find $x$ such that $\\log_{\\sqrt{2}} 8 = \\log_x 125$.\nTo solve for \\(x\\) in the equation \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\), we can use the change of base formula for logarithms. The change of base formula states that for any positive numbers \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) (with \\(a \\neq 1\\) and \\(c \\neq 1\\)):\n\n\\[\n\\log_a b = \\frac{\\log_c b}{\\log_c a}\n\\]\n\nWe can rewrite \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\) using the change of base formula. Let's choose a common base, say base 5, for simplicity. Then:\n\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{\\log_5 4}{\\log_5 x}\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log_5 3}{\\log_5 27}\n\\]\n\nGiven that \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\), we can write:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log_5 4}{\\log_5 x} = \\frac{\\log_5 3}{\\log_5 27}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\log_5 27 = \\log_5 (3^3) = 3 \\log_5 3\\), we can substitute this into the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log_5 4}{\\log_5 x} = \\frac{\\log_5 3}{3 \\log_5 3}\n\\]\n\nWe can cancel \\(\\log_5 3\\) from the numerator and the denominator on the right-hand side:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log_5 4}{\\log_5 x} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nThis equation implies that:\n\n\\[\n3 \\log_5 4 = \\log_5 x\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(\\log_5 4 = \\log_5 (2^2) = 2 \\log_5 2\\), so we can substitute this in:\n\n\\[\n3 (2 \\log_5 2) = \\log_5 x\n\\]\n\nSimplifying this, we get:\n\n\\[\n6 \\log_5 2 = \\log_5 x\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\n\\[\nx = (2^2)^6 = 2^{12} = 4096\n\\]\n\nSo, the value of \\(x\\) is \\(4096\\). Let's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Calculate the value of x\nx = 2 ** 12\nprint(x)\n```\n```output\n4096\n```\nThe value of \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{4096}\\).",
