      "text": "We start with the given equation: \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\).\n\nFirst, we express the logarithms in terms of the natural logarithm (or base 10): \n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{\\ln 4}{\\ln x} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\ln 3}{\\ln 27}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(27 = 3^3\\), we can express \\(\\ln 27\\) as:\n\\[\n\\ln 27 = \\ln (3^3) = 3 \\ln 3.\n\\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\ln 4}{\\ln x} = \\frac{\\ln 3}{3 \\ln 3}.\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify the right-hand side since \\(\\ln 3 \\neq 0\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{\\ln 4}{\\ln x} = \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(\\ln x\\), we multiply both sides by \\(\\ln x\\):\n\\[\n\\ln 4 = \\frac{1}{3} \\ln x.\n\\]\n\nNext, we solve for \\(\\ln x\\) by multiplying both sides by 3:\n\\[\n3 \\ln 4 = \\ln x.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\ln 4 = \\ln 2^2\\), we know that:\n\\[\n3 \\ln 4 = 3 \\ln (2^2) = 3 \\cdot 2 \\ln 2 = 6 \\ln 2.\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\n\\ln x = 6 \\ln 2.\n\\]\n\nExponentiating both sides of the equation (\\(e^x\\)) with respect to base \\(e\\), we obtain: \n\\[\nx = e^{6 \\ln 2}.\n\\]\n\nRecall the property of exponents and logarithms: \\(e^{\\ln a} = a\\). Therefore:\n\\[\ne^{6 \\ln 2} = (e^{\\ln 2})^6 = 2^6 = 64.\n\\]\n\nFinally, we obtain the value of \\(x\\):\n\\[\n\\boxed{64}.\n\\]",
